{"sections":[],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"extension","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Graph","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P"}],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"isPlanar","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"planarEmbedding()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"isOuterplanar","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" run the left-right planarity test (de","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Fraysseix and Rosenstiehl, in Brandes’s formulation, 2009); ","type":"text"},{"code":"findKuratowskiSubgraph()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" runs","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Boyer and Myrvold’s edge-addition algorithm (2004), whose failure state isolates the witness.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"All are O(n + m), iterative, and deterministic: graphs with the same rows (the same vertices in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the same order, the same edge positions, the same incidence order) get the same embedding and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the same witness.","type":"text"}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph"]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Graph","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"Graph","fragments":[{"text":"extension","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Graph","kind":"identifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P"}],"role":"symbol","roleHeading":"Extended Protocol","externalID":"s:e:s:14GraphProtocols0A0P9PlanarityE16_planarEdgeLimitSivp","extendedModule":"GraphProtocols","modules":[{"relatedModules":["GraphProtocols"],"name":"Planarity"}],"symbolKind":"extension"},"topicSections":[{"title":"Instance Properties","anchor":"Instance-Properties","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isOuterplanar","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isPlanar"],"generated":true},{"title":"Instance Methods","anchor":"Instance-Methods","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/findKuratowskiSubgraph()","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/planarEmbedding()"],"generated":true}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Planarity on undirected graphs. Self-loops and parallel edges never change an answer: the tests"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"run on the underlying simple graph, read once through the rows in index space. A directed"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"graph’s planarity is that of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"graph.undirected"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols"]]},"references":{"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols":{"type":"topic","title":"GraphProtocols","identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols","role":"collection","abstract":[],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/isPlanar":{"url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/isplanar","identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isPlanar","title":"isPlanar","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"isPlanar","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:Sb","text":"Bool","kind":"typeIdentifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"Whether the graph can be drawn in the plane without crossing edges (NetworkX’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"is_planar"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"boyer_myrvold_planarity_test"},{"text":"). A simple graph with more than 3n − 6 edges is not,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"which is answered after counting distinct pairs, before any search. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/planarEmbedding()":{"url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/planarembedding()","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/planarEmbedding()","kind":"symbol","title":"planarEmbedding()","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"A planar embedding of the graph, over every edge position (loops and parallel copies","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"included), or nil when the graph is not planar: the witness of planarity (NetworkX’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"check_planarity"},{"text":", Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"embedding"},{"text":" output). O(n + m), faces included.","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"planarEmbedding","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:9Planarity15PlanarEmbeddingV","text":"PlanarEmbedding","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">?","kind":"text"}]},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Graph":{"title":"Graph","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/graph","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Graph","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Graph","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"An undirected graph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of undirected edges"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"between them. Kept apart from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"type":"text","text":": an undirected graph is not a symmetric directed"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one, and no type is both. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"directed"},{"type":"text","text":" views it as one when an algorithm needs arcs."}],"type":"topic"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","abstract":[{"text":"Planarity on undirected graphs. Self-loops and parallel edges never change an answer: the tests","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"run on the underlying simple graph, read once through the rows in index space. A directed","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"graph’s planarity is that of ","type":"text"},{"code":"graph.undirected","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"Graph","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Graph","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"extension","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P","text":"Graph","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph","type":"topic"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/findKuratowskiSubgraph()":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/findKuratowskiSubgraph()","abstract":[{"text":"A subdivision of K₅ or K₃,₃ made of the graph’s edges, or nil when the graph is planar: the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"witness of non-planarity (Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"kuratowski_subgraph"},{"text":", JGraphT’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"getKuratowskiSubdivision()"},{"text":"). A dense graph is searched on its first 3n − 5 distinct pairs","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"only, which already hold one. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"findKuratowskiSubgraph()","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"findKuratowskiSubgraph","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"KuratowskiSubgraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:9Planarity18KuratowskiSubgraphV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">?","kind":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/findkuratowskisubgraph()","type":"topic"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity","abstract":[],"kind":"symbol","title":"Planarity","role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/planarity","type":"topic"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/isOuterplanar":{"type":"topic","title":"isOuterplanar","fragments":[{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"isOuterplanar","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:Sb","text":"Bool","kind":"typeIdentifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isOuterplanar","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Whether the graph has a planar embedding with every vertex on one face (Mathematica’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"OuterplanarGraphQ"},{"type":"text","text":"): whether the graph plus a new vertex joined to every vertex is planar."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"A simple graph with more than 2n − 3 edges is not. O(n + m)."}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/isouterplanar"}}}